16- * Axis-Aligned Bounding Box. An AABB is commonly used for fast overlap tests in collision
17- * detection, spatial indexing and frustum culling.
18- *
19- * A box is stored as a {@link center} and {@link halfExtents}. Set it from its extreme corners with
20: * {@link setMinMax} and read them back with {@link getMin} and {@link getMax}. Fit a box to vertex
21- * data with {@link compute}, grow it to enclose another box with {@link add}, and move a local box
22- * into world space with {@link setFromTransformedAabb}, which is how the engine derives a mesh
23- * instance's world bounds from its mesh's local bounds.
24- *
25- * Tests such as {@link intersects}, {@link containsPoint} and {@link intersectsRay} return a
26- * boolean and allocate nothing. {@link closestPoint} writes into an optional result vector, while
27- * {@link getMin} and {@link getMax} return the box's own cached vectors, which should be treated as
28- * read-only. The constructor copies the vectors it is given.
29- *
30- * @example
31- * // Enclose every mesh instance of a render component in one box
32- * const bounds = new BoundingBox();
